<BaseLayout
  title="401(k) Limits When Changing Jobs Midyear in 2027"
  description="The 2027 $25,500 401(k) limit is shared across all employers. Learn how to enter prior-employer YTD contributions and avoid an excess deferral."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>401(k) Limits When Changing Jobs Midyear in 2027</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> The $25,500 employee elective deferral limit ($34,000 at 50+, $37,250 at 60–63) is an <strong>individual per-person limit</strong> across all 401(k) plans in 2027, not a per-employer limit.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Enter Prior-Employer YTD in the Calculator →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Why Your New Employer Won’t Auto-Stop at $25,500</h2>
    <p>
      Your new employer’s payroll system only sees what you have contributed at your <em>new</em> job. It does not know how much you contributed at your prior employer earlier in 2027. If you contributed $10,000 at Job A and let Job B’s payroll run to $25,500, you will have a $10,000 excess deferral.
    </p>

    <h2>How to Set Your New Paycheck Amount</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Check your final pay stub from your prior employer for 2027 Box 12 (Code D for pre-tax, Code AA for Roth) elective deferrals.</li>
      <li>Open section <strong>C. Changed jobs or starting midyear?</strong> in the calculator and enter that amount in <strong>Prior employer in 2027</strong>.</li>
      <li>Confirm your exact number of remaining 2027 paychecks at your new job to get your adjusted per-paycheck amount.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

